findings.description:
`_append_step` appends without checking for an existing entry. Once a loop is re-entered and pauses again (likely, per the previous finding), `completed_steps` holds several entries for the same `step_name`. Their iterations may differ, and statuses may mix, e.g. `paused`, then `failed`, then `completed`.

The design doesn't specify:

- which entry `resume_iteration_for` reads (it should presumably be the last one);
- what it returns when the latest entry has `iteration` of 0 or missing (older runs);
- whether the Part A predicate should judge doneness by the *latest* entry rather than "any entry with a non-resumable status".

As written, a set built from any completed entry gives the right answer for paused-then-completed. It gives the wrong answer for completed-then-failed, which can happen if a step is re-run under `--from`. Specify "latest entry wins" for both functions and test the multi-entry case.
